{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgajDj7zGbE9tqx6aDnAV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I proved `revAcc_nil`, and `lean Main.lean` now finishes with no errors or warnings.\n\nInduction directly on the theorem doesn't work, because the accumulator stops being `[]` after the first step. So I added a more general helper lemma to `Main.lean` and proved that first:\n\n```lean\ntheorem revAcc_eq (xs acc : List α) : revAcc xs acc = myRev xs ++ acc := by\n  induction xs generalizing acc with\n  | nil => simp [revAcc, myRev]\n  | cons x xs ih => simp [revAcc, myRev, ih, List.append_assoc]\n\ntheorem revAcc_nil (xs : List α) : revAcc xs [] = myRev xs := by\n  simp [revAcc_eq]\n```\n\n- **`revAcc_eq`** says that running `revAcc` with any accumulator gives the reversed list followed by that accumulator. It's proved by induction on `xs`, with the accumulator allowed to vary at each step.\n- **`revAcc_nil`** is then the case where the accumulator is `[]`, since adding an empty list to the end changes nothing."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_read_input_tokens":7960,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":652},"output_tokens":377,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f5a1ad0e-c3c9-47da-b723-0e257f231aa5","uuid":"5979abac-ac48-46e2-acd4-e490b1a5d03c","timestamp":"2026-10-31T17:43:11.733Z","request_id":"req_011CgajDimfdxtpvYNPg6YDY"}
